1. Core Functional Domains
1.1 Payment Initiation & Processing

What it must do:

Accept payment requests across all flows (buy self, buy other, till-to-till, scratch card, E-TopUp)
Generate a unique, idempotent transaction ID before any external API call — never after
Trigger STK Push within ~1–2s of request (Daraja API latency is the bottleneck, not your code)
Handle the "user didn't respond to STK prompt" case — Safaricom will eventually timeout the push (~60–90s); you need a corresponding timeout state on your end, not an infinite "pending"

Performance expectation: STK Push initiation response to client: under 3s. If Daraja is slow, don't make the user wait — return "processing" immediately and let status polling/webhook handle the rest.

1.2 Webhook / Callback Reliability

What it must do:

Receive Daraja callbacks on a dedicated, always-available endpoint (not behind auth — Safaricom can't log in)
Verify payload authenticity/source before trusting it (IP allowlist or signature check, per Daraja docs)
Process idempotently — the same callback arriving twice (which happens) must not double-dispense airtime
Persist the raw payload before processing, so nothing is ever lost even if your processing logic throws

Performance expectation: Callback acknowledged (200 response to Safaricom) in under 500ms. Do the actual heavy processing (dispensing airtime, updating status) asynchronously via a queue job — don't make Safaricom wait on your reseller API call.

This is where BullMQ earns its place: callback comes in → validate → enqueue a job → return 200 immediately → job worker handles dispensing + retries independently.

1.3 Reconciliation (the part most MVPs skip, and enterprise systems don't)

What it must do:

Run a background job every 1–5 minutes scanning for transactions stuck in stk_sent or paid beyond expected thresholds
Actively query Daraja's Transaction Status API for those stuck transactions rather than waiting for a callback that may never arrive
Flag transactions that are inconsistent (e.g., Daraja says paid, but airtime dispensing failed) for either auto-retry or human review

Why this matters: callbacks fail to arrive a meaningful percentage of the time in real-world M-Pesa integrations — network issues, Safaricom-side delays, server restarts during the callback window. An enterprise system doesn't treat "wait for webhook" as the only path to truth.

1.4 Airtime/Card Dispensing

What it must do:

Call the reseller/telco API only after payment is confirmed (via callback OR reconciliation — either source of truth)
Retry on transient failures (network blip, reseller API 5xx) with exponential backoff, capped at N attempts
On repeated failure, don't leave the user's money in limbo — either auto-refund or escalate to a manual-refund queue visible in the admin dashboard
Record dispensing confirmation (reseller's own transaction/reference ID) for audit and dispute resolution

Performance expectation: dispensing attempt within 5–10s of payment confirmation under normal conditions.

1.5 Till-to-Till / Manual Code Redemption

What it must do:

Validate the pasted code against Daraja's Transaction Status API (or your Stratum reconciliation feed) — never trust regex-parsed text alone as authorization
Enforce single-use at the database level (unique constraint on the code), not just application logic, to survive race conditions (two requests submitting the same code near-simultaneously)
Rate-limit submission attempts per user/IP to prevent code-guessing abuse
1.6 Status Polling API

What it must do:

Return current, uncached state on every request (Cache-Control: no-store, no ETags)
Support both polling (simple, works everywhere) and, longer-term, WebSocket/SSE push for lower latency and reduced server load at scale
Auto-expire/stop client-side polling after a reasonable window (60–90s), falling back to "we'll notify you" messaging
1.7 AI Assistant Layer

Support chat:

Tool-call into your own transaction/status data — never let the model answer "where's my airtime" from imagination
Clear escalation to human support for disputes, fraud claims, refund requests

SMS/code parsing:

AI extracts structured fields from pasted M-Pesa text (convenience layer only)
Daraja/Stratum API remains the sole source of truth for authorization — the AI never authorizes a payout by itself
1.8 Admin / Ops Dashboard

What it must do:

Real-time transaction feed (all flows, filterable by status/network/date)
Manual intervention tools: force-retry dispensing, manual refund, mark resolved
Float/balance monitoring per network — alert before you run out of stock to fulfill orders
Redeemed-code audit trail with flagged duplicate attempts
Callback health metrics — success rate, latency, failure spikes (an early warning system for Safaricom-side issues)
1.9 Security & Compliance

What it must do:

All secrets (Daraja keys, DB credentials, reseller API keys) in environment variables/secrets manager
Rate limiting on every public endpoint, stricter on payment-initiation and code-redemption
Audit logging on every state-changing action, with actor (user/system/admin) and timestamp
PII handling: phone numbers and transaction data should be access-controlled, not queryable by just anyone with DB access
2. Non-Functional / Performance Targets
Metric	Target
STK Push initiation response	< 3s
Callback acknowledgment	< 500ms
Airtime dispensing after confirmed payment	< 10s (normal conditions)
Status endpoint response	< 200ms
Reconciliation job frequency	every 1–5 min
Uptime target	99.5%+ (paid DB tier, proper process management, no free-tier flapping)
Callback duplicate-processing rate	0% (must be mathematically impossible via idempotency keys, not just "unlikely")
3. What Separates "Working Demo" from "Enterprise-Grade" Here
Idempotency everywhere — every state-changing endpoint (payment init, callback, redemption, dispensing) must be safe to call twice with the same key and produce the same result, not a duplicate side effect.
Reconciliation, not just webhooks — treating the webhook as the only source of truth is the #1 way these systems silently lose money or strand users in "pending" forever.
Queue-based async processing — anything slow (external API calls) happens off the request/response cycle, via BullMQ jobs with retries, not inline in a route handler.
Observability — structured logs keyed by transaction ID, error tracking (Sentry), and dashboards showing callback success rates and dispensing failure rates in real time, not just console.log output in a terminal you have to be watching.
Database-level guarantees, not just application logic — unique constraints, foreign keys, and transactions (DB transactions, not payment transactions) enforcing correctness even if application code has bugs or races.
Graceful degradation — when a downstream dependency (Daraja, reseller API) is slow or down, the system queues and retries rather than failing the user's request outright or hanging indefinitely.
4. Suggested Build Order
Core payment flow (self/other) end-to-end, with proper idempotency and status polling — get this rock-solid before adding more flows
Reconciliation job — don't skip this, even in v1
Till-to-till redemption with DB-level duplicate prevention
Admin dashboard (basic feed + manual override is enough for v1)
Scratch cards / E-TopUp
AI support chat
AI SMS parsing
Observability/monitoring polish, load testing, then move off any free-tier infra before real launch